{"t":1795346419,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"316f6540-2440-429f-b2e7-fffe7e5b788e","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-11-22T08:03:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 220803\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n303 AM EST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nHave pushed back the timing of rainfall today given substantial\nlow-level dry air across the region. The system Wednesday into\nThanksgiving Day looks primed to bring rain and windy\nconditions, but details are still uncertain given continued\nmodel spread.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Periods of rain through tonight with a coastal low before\n  drier weather returns early next week.\n\n- 2) Cooler conditions are expected next week, with a coastal\n  low forecast to track up the East Coast on Thanksgiving Day.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Periods of rain through tonight with a coastal\nlow before drier weather returns early next week.\n\nRegional radar imagery across the Mid-Atlantic shows light\nreturns lifting northward across the area. However, much of this\nis likely struggling to reach the ground given substantial low-\nlevel dry air. Local aircraft soundings show a pocket of dry air\nsitting between 850-650 mb. Some moistening is now underway well\nsouth of I-66 as skies have become overcast in the 2,200 to\n3,000 foot level. Looking down toward the Carolinas, this is\nwhere the more impressive rainfall signature is, particularly\ntoward the North Carolina-South Carolina coastal border. Models\ngenerally agree on light activity continuing to lift northward\nthroughout the morning. It remains to be seen if any of this\nmakes it to the surface though.\n\nOvercast skies will be prevalent today while winds gradually\nturn more north to northeasterly. This is in response to the\napproach of a wavy frontal system which straddles the Carolina\nto Delmarva coast. Despite the rain chances today, forecast\nhighs should reach the low/mid 60s, with 50s over mountain\nlocations. In terms of the expected rainfall, there has\ncertainly been a downward trend in forecast amounts. At this\npoint, areas east of the Blue Ridge can expect 0.10 to 0.25\ninches, with even lighter amounts across the Shenandoah Valley.\nThe 00Z REFS ensembles show the most concentrated activity \ncrossing the I-95 metro areas between around 6-10 PM this \nevening before shifting toward the Eastern Shore after midnight.\n\nAs the wavy coastal system pulls out to sea, a cold front\npressing across the Mississippi Valley will reach the area on\nMonday morning. While most stay dry, some northwesterly aided\nupslope rain showers are possible along/west of the Allegheny\nFront. Further to the east, the resultant downsloping flow will\nmake for one more mild day as highs push into the mid 60s to low\n70s. The cool spots will be across the mountains with Monday's\nhighs in the upper 40s to 50s. Model guidance shows a more\npronounced drop in theta-e values which comes with falling\ntemperatures and dew points. Additionally, this comes with a\nsurge of northwesterly winds with evening gusts up to 20 to 25\nmph. A seasonably chilly night lies ahead with lows in the\nmid/upper 20s west of the Blue Ridge, to widespread 30s off to\nthe east.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Cooler conditions are expected next week, with a\ncoastal low forecast to track up the East Coast on Thanksgiving\nDay.\n\nAfter a chilly couple of days Tue-Wed under strong Canadian high \npressure, the next low pressure area to impact the area on \nThanskgiving Day has trended slower, closer to the coast, wetter, \nand windier according to the latest set of ensemble guidance. \n\nThe parent 500 mb trough has trended deeper with a closed 500 mb low \nforecast to develop over the Southeast U.S. coast Thanksgiving Day, \nlifting to the VA Tidewater region by Thu evening. This results in \nthe associated low pressure center to lift north significantly \nslower than in previous runs resulting in stronger winds along the \ncoast and heavier precip with less cold air available in the \nmountains for accumulating snow. The system looks to lift northeast \nof the area by late Thu night or early Fri with precip ending. A \nbrief period of upslope flow and cold temperatures may support light \nsnow showers across the mountains Fri, but favorable cyclonic flow \nlooks to last too short in duration for any appreciable snow \namounts. Weak high pressure builds over the area for next weekend \nwith seasonable temperatures for late November.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /06Z SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nInitial low-level dry air across the region has delayed any\nrelated restrictions today. However, as noted at KCHO (3,000\nfoot overcast), this moistening will gradually take place today\nwhich favors borderline MVFR/VFR ceilings (right around 2,500 to\n3,000 feet). Light rain overspreads the area late this afternoon\nwhile continuing through the evening. A wavy frontal system\noffshore will favor a shift to light northeasterlies today. Any\nsub-VFR conditions should abate by early Monday as a cold front\ntracks through from the west. VFR conditions are expected for\nMonday and Tuesday with mainly northwesterly winds. Afternoon\ngusts each day should peak around 20 to 25 knots.\n\nNo sig wx is expected Wed under high pressure. Rain is expected to \nmove late Wed night with the next area of low pressure and continue \nthrough Thanskgiving Day. Flight restrictions appear likely along \nwith a threat of gusty winds as high as 25-30 kt.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nCurrent north to northeasterly winds remain light early this\nmorning, generally peaking around 5 to 10 knots in a few spots.\nWith the wavy coastal system being weaker in nature, any threat\nfor Small Craft Advisories will occur in a limited window\nstarting late this afternoon. Near 20 knot gusts are possible \nacross the more southern waters between 3-7 PM. A cold front\nmoves through early Monday which favors prevailing\nnorthwesterlies which eventually turn more northerly by late\nMonday night. A secondary surge moving across the waters will\nlikely require Small Craft Advisories Monday evening into much\nof Tuesday. Wind gusts peak around 25 to 30 knots on Monday\nnight into early Tuesday morning.\n\nPotential for gale force winds seems to be increasing for the Wed-\nThu night time frame, first under NE flow before the low pressure \ncenter approaches the area, then in N to NNW flow as the low \npressure center departs the region. \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nA much moister environment moves into the entire region today as \nan area of low pressure lifts up the Carolina coast. Winds pick up\nintensity early next week behind a strong cold front. This brings\nin cooler weather, but also another round of reduced daily \nhumidities. Another coastal system may bring wet and windy \nconditions to the region by the middle of next week, which could \ninclude a chance for mountain snow. This looks to continue into \nthe Thanksgiving holiday with very gusty winds possible across the\nregion.\n\n&&\n\n.TIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...\nRelatively light winds through Monday may allow for tides to\nelevate somewhat, resulting in action stages at vulnerable sites\nlike Annapolis, particularly late Monday afternoon. Thereafter,\nstrong offshore flow will result in decreasing tides Monday\nnight into Tuesday.\n\nAnother coastal low pressure area will likely move up the East\nCoast Wednesday into Thursday. Depending on the evolution and\ntrack, tide levels may rise once again.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...None.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...LFR/BRO\nAVIATION...LFR/BRO\nMARINE...LFR/BRO\nFIRE WEATHER...BRO\nTIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...LWX\n"},"forecast":{"t":1795346361,"days":{"2026-11-22":{"hi":66,"lo":52,"pop":53,"short":"Chance Light Rain